{%- comment -%}
  Resolves the one collection a product should say it belongs to, and echoes its
  title as plain text. Case is left to the caller — the PDP's two eyebrows both
  uppercase in CSS, but a caller that needs the filter can still apply it.

  Why this exists: `product.collections.first` is not a merchant decision.
  Shopify does not promise an order, every product sits in ALL, and most also
  sit in the Faire tracking collection — so the label was effectively random and
  could show a collection no shopper should ever see.

  Order of preference:
    1. custom.main_collection — the collection picked on the product in admin
    2. the first collection whose handle is not on the ignore list
    3. the fallback label

  Both the ignore list and the fallback are theme settings, so the rule is
  merchandising, not code.

  Usage:
    {%- capture main_collection %}{% render 'main-collection', product: product %}{% endcapture -%}
    {%- assign main_collection = main_collection | strip -%}
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign main = product.metafields.custom.main_collection.value

  if main != blank and main.title != blank
    echo main.title
  else
    assign label = settings.collection_label_fallback | default: 'ALL'

    # `contains` over a split array is exact membership, not a substring test,
    # so the handles have to be normalised before the comparison.
    assign ignore_raw = settings.collection_label_ignore | default: 'all-products'
    assign ignore = ignore_raw | downcase | replace: ' ', '' | split: ','

    for c in product.collections
      unless ignore contains c.handle
        assign label = c.title
        break
      endunless
    endfor

    echo label
  endif
-%}
